+# September 11 DOT crisis-center reports and the Mineta office call-log search
+
+November 9, 2026 Eastern · B129 / PD01–PD04 and Mineta operational-record acquisition.
+
+On October 10, 2001, the administrator of the Research and Special Programs Administration (RSPA) told the Senate that DOT's Crisis Management Center prepared situation reports for the Secretary after the September 11 attacks. Her prepared statement identifies the **Activation Information Management System (AIM)** and describes reports prepared hourly or more frequently. The 2005 DOT rule identifies the office that took over crisis-center operations. Together these are specific leads to a reporting system, records to look for, and the offices that may have kept copies. [Senate hearing, printed pp. 9 and 11](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-107shrg89502/pdf/CHRG-107shrg89502.pdf) · [DOT final rule, 70 FR 7669–7670](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2005-02-15/pdf/05-2803.pdf)
+
+## What the records say
+
+| Source and locator | Observation | Limit |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Engleman oral testimony, hearing printed p. 9, HTML preformatted lines 681–697 | The activation order came “Within 30 minutes” of the first WTC attack. The center sat in RSPA's Office of Emergency Transportation (OET) and produced immediate situation reports for the Secretary. | An account of the order and reporting, with exact order time and first report time open. |
+| Engleman prepared statement, hearing printed p. 11, HTML preformatted lines 811–825 | The order came “In less than 30 minutes.” The account identifies AIM as the reporting system and adds the hourly or more frequent report cadence. | Preserve the timing language and cadence as stated. The two statements share an author and furnish no independent clock measurements. |
+| DOT final rule, docket OST–1999–6189, 70 FR 7669–7670 | Emergency transportation operations moved from RSPA to the Office of the Secretary, with authority delegated to the renamed Office of Intelligence, Security, and Emergency Response. The rule states **February 4, 2005** as its effective date and appears in the **February 15, 2005** issue. | The rule establishes operational responsibility. The transfer of 2001 report files, AIM records or backups requires a separate inventory or accession. |
+
+[Hearing HTML](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/CHRG-107shrg89502/html/CHRG-107shrg89502.htm) · [Source hashes and reproducible locators](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+The reporting system and reports are separate targets from the office telephone logs named in OST FOIA request **2007-144 / 2007-0144**. The earlier export records **March 13, 2008** as that request's close date. The final response and enclosures remain outstanding. This pass acquired neither a situation report nor an AIM export. A future report could bound when specific information was recorded or circulated if its time base, versions and distribution can be established. Attributing a call would require a matching event or telephone record. [Earlier FOIA-export comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/6bae0ba697ee0d560545a509963b73fd90f4f5f0/investigation/broader-evidence/faa-ost-foia-and-correspondence-2026-11-09/README.md)
+
+## San José State collection descriptions
+
+| Public description | Field as displayed or extracted | Scope relevant to the search |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| SJSU ScholarWorks, MSS-1996-02-17, uploaded 2009 | “six series” | Series VI Transportation Files end in **1995**. Series IV Public Relations & Press Files extend to **2001**. The page warns that its guide may be out of date. |
+| OAC, MSS-1996-02-17, guide encoded June 3, 2025 | “nine series” and “366 boxes (368.72 linear feet)” | Series VI likewise ends in **1995**. Series VII photographic and visual records extend to **2006**, while the collection's summary dates end in **2001**. |
+| House historical volume, manuscript collections, physical p. 9 / printed p. 384 | “435.3 linear feet” | Its SJSU entry expressly lists files from the U.S. Department of Transportation, without a series or box locator for the office logs. |
+| OAC, MSS-2014-09-10, separate Secretary Norman Y. Mineta Papers | Dates **1995-2015, bulk 2006-2013**. Scope text **1995-2013 (bulk 2006-2013)**. | The guide emphasizes work after the 2006 resignation. Its administrative series is dated **2006-2013**. |
+
+[SJSU ScholarWorks guide](https://scholarworks.sjsu.edu/speccoll_archives/50/) · [OAC legislative collection](https://oac.cdlib.org/findaid/ark:/13030/kt2n39r8jd) · [House historical volume, p. 9](https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GPO-CDOC-108hdoc226/pdf/GPO-CDOC-108hdoc226-2-4-7.pdf#page=9) · [OAC Secretary collection](https://oac.cdlib.org/findaid/ark:/13030/c8j38wzv)
+
+These descriptions leave the relationship between the extents, series counts and dates unresolved. The two downloaded OAC overview responses have collection-contents headings without item-level inventories in the response text. The review covers the catalog descriptions only. No box, folder or item for the office logs was identified. The House entry's DOT wording is a retrieval lead rather than a verified location for the logs.
+
+## Open record and test queue
+
+1. **Office calls:** The final OST response associated with the March 13, 2008 close date, its routing and search records, enclosures and any telephone-log originals or exports. Preserve the literal request identifiers.
+2. **Crisis center:** The September 11 AIM event history, earliest situation reports and revisions, activation order and timestamp, and distribution or receipt evidence. Look for originating OET copies, Secretary-office recipient copies, and any records-transfer or migration inventory for the 2005 handoff. The current custody and retention of the 2001 records remain open.
+3. **Comparison once records arrive:** Distinguish event time from entry, revision, dispatch and receipt times. Record the stated clocks and coverage, then compare specific events against independently anchored records. No activation minute, call match or timing discrepancy has been established by this pass.
+4. **FAA correction and disposition:** The corrected May 2003 answer, its acknowledgment, the signed action or no-action determination and the separate recommendation outcomes remain open. The AIM and call-log searches concern September 11 operations and do not substitute for those later administrative records.
+
+Six public source entities were pinned. The reviewed scope covers the two testimony passages, the relevant two-page rule, the House manuscript-collection entry and collection-level guide fields. The checks establish source identity and field readings within that scope.
